@startuml

salt
{
  <b>ORE STUDIO - REPORTING: CREATE A REPORT</b>
  --
  New definition | Party ACMCOR | Tenant administrator
  --
  <b>THE RAIL: ONE ENTRY PER STEP, IN THE ORDER THE STEPS DECLARE</b>
  {#
    Mark | Step | State
    Y | What it is | done
    2 | What it reads | current
    3 | What it runs | ahead
    4 | Review and save | ahead
  }
  The current entry carries aria-current step. The last entry is final. The rail
  is drawn from the step list, so the rail and the steps cannot disagree, and it
  marks position only: it cannot say how many items a save would refuse.
  --
  <b>STEP 1 - WHAT IT IS</b>
  --
  {#
    Field | Value
    Name | Intraday Exposure
    Description | Exposure profiles
    Report type | risk
    Is official | yes
    Concurrency policy | skip
  }
  The report type is a combo box over ores_reporting_report_types_tbl. risk is
  the one type registered today and it names report_execution_workflow. More
  types are expected, so the control reads the table rather than a fixed pair.
  The party comes from the session and is not a choice.
  --
  <b>STEP 2 - WHAT IT READS</b>
  --
  {#
    Configuration type | Screen that holds its data
    conventions | PATHS.conventions
    pricing_engines | no screen exists
    todays_market | no screen exists
    curve_configuration | no screen exists
    risk_report_config | no screen exists
  }
  A risk report binds all five configuration types. Each row states what its
  configuration supplies: the conventions the trades are read with, the pricing
  model of each product, the market data a run prices against, the curves with
  their bootstrap and quotes, and the run settings the party shares. Each row
  also names the screen that holds the data, and only conventions has one, at
  /refdata/conventions. The other four are stated as missing rather than drawn
  as links.
  {
    [ Bind a configuration ]
  }
  The binding is written with report_configurations.put, from the candidates
  configurations.list_by_configuration_type_code returns. The portfolio scope is
  Group Treasury Portfolio, carried by the bound risk report config, and no
  operation in this journey's set writes it.
  --
  <b>STEP 3 - WHAT IT RUNS</b>
  --
  {#
    On | Analytic | Order | Settings
    Y | npv | 1 | discount curve
    Y | simulation | 2 | paths, grid
    Y | pfe | 3 | horizon, quantile
    N | saccr | 4 | row kept
    | eleven more types | | one row each
  }
  Fourteen analytic types, each with an active toggle and a display_order. An
  analytic is turned off by setting active false; nothing is deleted, and its
  settings stay readable.
  {
    [ Add an analytic ]
  }
  Adding one is the flow Change what a report runs carries: the list of the
  fourteen types, then the detail with what the analytic is, how it is
  calculated, and every parameter with a control chosen for its shape and a note
  on what it means, then the effect of turning it on.
  --
  <b>REFUSED: pfe WITHOUT simulation</b>
  --
  pfe reads the cube that simulation writes, so a definition that enables pfe
  without simulation cannot work. The rule lives in the ACME seeder, in
  check_exposure_needs_simulation, and not in the reporting service. Nothing
  validates it when a definition is written: the flow refuses it, and the server
  would accept it.
  --
  <b>STEP 4 - REVIEW AND SAVE</b>
  --
  {#
    Written | Operation
    definition | report_definitions.put
    five binds | report_configurations.put
    analytic rows | configuration_analytics.put_many
    parameter rows | configuration_analytic_parameters.put
    the scope | no operation
  }
  Every field value is named, including the ones that stay empty: name Intraday
  Exposure, report_type risk, is_official true, concurrency_policy skip,
  schedule_expression none, scheduler_job_id none, fsm_state_id none,
  run_grant_id none, pre_processing execute, prepared_input_key null,
  post_processing execute. Also the analytics with their active and
  display_order, their parameter values, and the portfolio scope the bound
  configuration carries.
  {
    [ Save the report ]
  }
  The read-back states the stored row with report_definitions.get, that the
  definition is on demand, and that it has never run.
  This step is final, so its foot carries no Back and the rail marks it final.
  Going back is refused at the first step and when the step before the current
  one is final.
  --
  <b>WHAT THIS SCREEN CANNOT SHOW</b>
  --
  No operation answers what a report needs in order to run beyond the
  configuration types its report type requires. Nothing validates the
  pfe-needs-simulation rule on write. No operation writes the portfolio scope.
  Four of the five configuration types have no screen. No BFF route serves
  reporting, and nothing in the application creates a report today.
}

@enduml
